"The disjointed letters (huruf al-muqatta'at: alif-lam-ra) at the beginning of Surah Ibrahim are the sounds that constitute the miraculous composition of the Quran, and these letters manifest as evidence of tawhid and truthfulness. This book was revealed by Allah to the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) so that he might bring people out of darkness into light, onto the path of Allah, the Almighty, the Praiseworthy. Allah is the owner of whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth; He is the Lord not only of those in the heavens, but also of those on earth. This book is a light to rescue people from misguidance and wretchedness and lead them to the light of faith; however, this guidance is realized only by Allah's leave. There is good news for the believers, and a severe punishment for the disbelievers.\n\nThe disbelievers prefer the worldly life to the Hereafter, turn others away from the path of Allah, and seek to make it crooked. Disliking truthfulness, they strive to lead people astray, and thus they are in deep misguidance. In response to those who object to this book being in Arabic, Allah declares that He sent every messenger in the language of his own people. Messengers convey the message first to their closest kin, and then it spreads to other languages. There are many wisdoms behind it being revealed in Arabic; had it been revealed in different languages, unity could not have been achieved and the need for translation would have increased. Allah leaves astray whom He wills and guides whom He wills; He is the Almighty, the All-Wise.\n\nMoses (Musa) was also sent with signs and was commanded to bring his people out of darkness into light. He was asked to remind them of the Days of Allah; in these days, there are lessons for everyone who is patient and grateful. Moses, reminding the Children of Israel of Allah's favor, told them that He had saved them from the oppression of Pharaoh, who slaughtered their sons and spared their women. Allah proclaims that if you give thanks, He will surely increase your blessings, but if you are ungrateful, His punishment is severe indeed. Moses stated that even if everyone were ungrateful, Allah is free of all needs and worthy of all praise.\n\nHas there not reached you the news of previous nations—the people of Noah, Ad, Thamud, and many others? None knows them except Allah. When their messengers came to them with clear miracles, they put their hands to their mouths in denial and doubted the call. Their messengers asked, 'Is there any doubt concerning Allah, the Creator of the heavens and the earth? He calls you so that He may forgive your sins and grant you respite until an appointed term.' The disbelievers said, 'You are nothing but human beings like us,' demanding clear proof. The messengers replied, 'We are indeed only human beings like you, but Allah bestows His grace upon whom He wills; we cannot bring you proof except by Allah's leave. And in Allah alone let the believers place their trust; they endure suffering.'\n\nThe disbelievers told their messengers that they would either expel them from their lands or force them to return to their religion. Allah, however, revealed that He would destroy the wrongdoers and settle the believers in the land after them. The messengers sought victory, and every obstinate tyrant met with ruin. Ahead of him is Hell, where he will be made to drink purulent water; he will try to sip it but will hardly be able to swallow it, death will come at him from every side yet he will not die. The deeds of those who disbelieve in the Most Merciful are like ashes blown away by the wind on a stormy day; they can gain nothing from what they have earned. That is deep misguidance.\n\nAllah created the heavens and the earth in truth; if He wills, He can destroy you and bring forth a new creation. This is not difficult for Allah. On the Day of Resurrection, everyone will appear before Allah; the weak will say to those who were arrogant, 'We were your followers, so can you avert from us anything of the punishment?' They will reply, 'Had Allah guided us, we would have guided you; it is all the same to us now whether we panic or remain patient, there is no place of escape for us.' When the matter is concluded, Satan will say: 'Allah made you a true promise, and I promised you and failed you; I had no power over you except that I called you, and you responded to me. So do not blame me, blame yourselves. I cannot save you, nor can you save me.' For the wrongdoers, there is a painful punishment. As for those who believe and do righteous deeds, they will be admitted to gardens, where they will be greeted with peace.\n\nAllah compares a good word to a good tree whose root is firmly fixed and whose branches reach into the sky; it yields its fruit at all times by the leave of its Lord. An evil word, however, is like an evil tree uprooted from the surface of the earth, having no stability. Allah keeps the believers firm on the steadfast word in this worldly life and in the Hereafter; He leads the wrongdoers astray, and Allah does whatever He wills. Those who exchange the favors of Allah for ingratitude and cause their people to settle in the abode of ruin will enter Hell. To those who set up rivals to Allah in order to lead others astray from His path, it is said, 'Enjoy yourselves, for your destination is the Fire.' The believers are commanded to establish prayer and spend from the provision given to them.\n\nAllah created the heavens and the earth, sent down water from the sky and brought forth fruits with it, subjected ships and rivers to your service, made the sun and the moon constant in their courses, subjected the night and the day to your service, and gave you of everything you asked of Him. If you were to count Allah's favors, you could never enumerate them; indeed, man is exceedingly unjust and ungrateful. Abraham (Ibrahim) prayed, 'My Lord! Make this city secure, and keep me and my sons away from worshipping idols.' Idols have led many people astray; whoever follows Abraham is of him, and whoever disobeys, Allah is Forgiving and Merciful. Abraham settled some of his offspring near the Sacred House (Bayt al-Haram) and asked that they establish prayer, be provided with fruits, and give thanks.\n\nAbraham praised Allah, stating that He knows both what is concealed and what is revealed, and that He had granted him Ishmael and Isaac in his old age. He prayed that his descendants would be among those who establish prayer, and asked for forgiveness for himself, his parents, and the believers on the Day of Reckoning. The Prophet was informed that Allah is not unaware of what the wrongdoers do, but only defers their punishment to a Day when eyes will stare in horror. On that Day, they will rush forward with their heads raised, their gazes never returning to them, and their hearts utterly void. The wrongdoers will plead, 'Grant us respite so that we may respond to Your call'; to them it will be said, 'Did you not swear before that you would never suffer demise?'\n\nYou dwelt in the dwellings of those who wronged themselves, and you saw how they were punished; and examples were presented to you. They devised their plots, but with Allah is the punishment for their plotting, even if their plot were such as to move mountains. Allah never breaks His promise to His messengers; He is Almighty, the Lord of Retribution. On that Day, the earth will be changed into another earth, and the heavens into other heavens; and everyone will be gathered before Allah, the One, the Irresistible. The criminals will be bound in chains, their garments of pitch, and fire covering their faces. Allah will recompense every soul according to what it earned; He is swift in reckoning. This Quran is a proclamation sent to mankind so that they may be warned, know that Allah is the One God, and that people of understanding may take heed.\n\nAllah the Almighty declares that on the Day of Resurrection, the earth and the heavens will be changed. Scholars have put forward different views regarding the nature of this change. Some stated that the earth will be completely annihilated and a new earth created, while others stated that the attributes of the same matter will change—that is, it will be transformed into another form. Ibn Mas'ud narrated that the earth will become like white silver, whereas Ibn Abbas narrated that the earth will remain the same, but the mountains will be set in motion and flattened out.\n\nThe issue of the annihilation or transformation of matter has also been discussed from philosophical and scientific perspectives. Some philosophers, claiming that matter is eternal, considered its annihilation impossible and accepted only its transformation. However, experiments in modern science showing that matter can be converted into energy have suggested that absolute annihilation might also be possible. Nevertheless, definitive knowledge on this matter belongs to Allah, and for believers, accepting either possibility is sufficient.\n\nOn the Day of Judgment, the criminals will be seen brought close together and bound in chains. Their garments will be made of pitch. Pitch is a black, foul-smelling, and easily flammable substance. This depiction illustrates the misery in which the criminals find themselves and the severity of the punishment. Some exegetes have stated that this pitch is actually their evil deeds and beliefs in this world, and that in the Hereafter, these deeds will materialize and turn into torment. Furthermore, fire will envelop their faces, which shows that the punishment of Hell will encompass both the inside and the outside.\n\nAllah will recompense everyone according to the deeds they have earned. This is the wisdom behind the changing of the heavens and the earth and the chaining of the criminals. Allah is swift in reckoning; being occupied with one matter does not distract Him from another. Everyone will receive good as a recompense for goodness and evil as a recompense for evil. This is a requirement of divine justice.\n\nThis surah is a proclamation, a warning, and an admonition to humanity. The purpose is for people to be warned, to know that Allah is One, and for people of understanding to reflect and take heed. The letter 'waw' at the end of the surah connects this verse both to the previous surah and prepares a transition to the subsequent Surah al-Hijr. In this respect, Surah Ibrahim is a summary emphasizing the principles of faith, while Surah al-Hijr serves as a message that complements it with an emphasis on warning and tawhid."